attachments that google didn't like?

INLA by default impose the zero-sum constraint, but no other
constraint.  If my earlier hypothesis is correct, the effect you see
in the INLA plot is rather due to other constructions in the internals
of the implementation, that have a similar effect to "observing" zeros
everywhere, but with very large variance (equivalent to adding a small
diagonal matrix to the prior precision matrix, which is usually done
to handle practical intrinsic model computations instead of costly
non-sparse eigenvector methods).  One would need to look at the very
technical details of the implementation to verify that.  The
alternative constraint imposition I outlined (fixing the endpoints)
should be much much less affected by this.

Finn

