Alright thanks a lot. The trick of adding small numbers to the diagonal of the precision matrix, inverting it, and sampling from the multivariate normal seems to be what is going on in INLA. I can now generate sample paths that look like INLA's by adding 1e-6 to the main diagonal of Q.

Thanks also for the pointer to extraconstr, this is likely to be useful at some point.

I'm a bit concerned about the strong dependence of the appearance of the sample paths on the value of the constant that is added to the diagonal. For example adding 1e-7 instead of 1e-6 (in my own code) doubles the marginal variance of the process. To assess my prior choices on process parameters, I often simulate from the prior to look at distributions of derived quantities for which I have genuine prior beliefs. If the prior simulations depend so strongly on the choice of a numerical fudge factor, I'm not sure whether I should be doing this in INLA. Maybe your suggestion of specifying the linear constraints solves the problem?

